WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.250
This was not a, what you would
consider a TV friendly set.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:05.280
Yeah, I would agree with you there.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.330
I'm not a TV friendly
person on average. (laughs)

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.330
You've got racism, homophobia,

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.110
sexual assault, imperialism,

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:13.320
critiquing your founding fathers,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.800
attacking the Disney corporation (laughs)

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.600
And then he tags these three genocides.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.019
They didn't bring that up.

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:21.290
(upbeat music)

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:22.123
[JP] A lot of work

00:00:22.123 --> 00:00:24.060
goes into a short late night stand-up set.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.670
Join me, JP Buck as I
spotlight the comedians

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.200
who came up with some of
my favorite point sets.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.510
This is, "The Set Up".

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.218
Please welcome the very
funny Solomon Georgio.

00:00:34.218 --> 00:00:37.080
(upbeat music)

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.210
Usually I have a story of like

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.770
the first time I saw this comedian,

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.330
and I don't really have
that for you because for me,

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.510
it was the first time I heard
the reaction to this comedian.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.800
Let's tell the story
from the beginning JP,

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.440
you were in the bathroom
while I was on stage.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.640
Back up a step, let's back it up a step.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.640
So, the meltdown County,
meltdown comics was a comedy,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.410
it was a was a comic book store

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:02.300
that Kumail Nanjiani and Jonah Ray

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.210
used to host a show every Wednesday

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:05.610
in the back storage room,

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:10.610
and it became the best
comedy night in Los Angeles.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.050
And it ran like that for number of years.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:13.883
Yes.

00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:14.810
Whatever I had seen, I was like,

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:15.650
I was going to take a break,

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.160
and so I stepped out to use the restroom,

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.680
and as it wasn't even 10
seconds after I closed the door

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.583
behind me that the audience lost it.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.520
And I just thought I've
got to get back out there

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.380
and see what the heck is going on.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:35.350
And I come out and it's
you destroying onstage.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.980
I got off the stage and I
think I went to the bathroom,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.680
came back and Emily Gordon,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.680
who also one of the producers
of the show at the time,

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:50.450
she was like, Hey Solomon, by the way,

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.730
the booker from Conan was here,

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:54.500
I'm like, uh-huh..

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.440
and he asked me for your email.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.140
And she asked me if that was okay,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.100
I was like, oh yeah,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.350
am I going to be the first
I'm going to be that comic

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.960
that's like, sorry,
who try to talk to you?

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:04.793
The booker for what show? (JP laughs)

00:02:04.793 --> 00:02:09.520
Because I was like, eight, nine
years in and it wasn't easy,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.000
and like I was like
barely making any money,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.830
and like that, that's the thing is like,

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.710
as a comic, there's a lot of ability

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.560
to being as a black
gay comic at that time,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.980
specifically, like I wasn't
being featured for anybody.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.830
I was getting college shows
and they were very sporadic,

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.420
but I was like, I'm like nine
years, almost 10 years in.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.700
You just kind of get in your
own head about whether or not

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.500
you should be doing this.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.390
And especially if you're
working as a server

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.320
with a college degree, and you're like,

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.630
what am I doing with my life?

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:41.140
And this was just a quintessential moment

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.220
that made me understand that
I am doing the right thing.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.730
I'm good at what I'm doing,

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:49.483
and I don't have to be a
server forever. (chuckles)

00:02:50.915 --> 00:02:53.915
(audience cheering)

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:01.600
Thank you.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:02.831
Hey everybody.

00:03:02.831 --> 00:03:04.510
(clapping continues)

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.060
I'll give you a moment to adjust.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.250
That was me going, "Holy, this is going-

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.232
this is happening right now."

00:03:11.232 --> 00:03:12.310
(JP laughs)

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.570
Like that walk from the
green room to the curtain,

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:19.030
I feel should be at least 20
minutes long of like people

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.250
in my life coming to
me and letting me know

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:22.330
it's going to be okay,

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:24.080
but it's all a 10 seconds (laughs)

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.218
It is.

00:03:25.218 --> 00:03:26.051
(JP laughs)

00:03:26.051 --> 00:03:27.160
Me running out was like,

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:28.060
feels like I look prepared,

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.250
but it was more along the lines of like,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.670
I'll give you a moment, which is like,

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.730
Oh, give me a second. I think
I'm going to die (chuckles)

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:38.010
My name is Solomon Georgio.
That is my real name.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.894
It's a very beautiful name
for a very beautiful man.

00:03:40.894 --> 00:03:42.820
(audience laughs)

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:47.008
My last name Gorgio is
Italian, I am Ethiopian.

00:03:47.008 --> 00:03:49.090
(audience laughs)

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:49.923
Some people wonder

00:03:49.923 --> 00:03:53.420
how does an African get
a European last name?

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:56.280
Well, (audience laughing)

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.773
it's a lot like a fairytale,

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:00.510
except in this fairytale,

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.860
there happens to be an
Italian army occupation,

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:05.540
a brutal civil war,

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.666
a few decades of famine
and no happy endings.

00:04:08.666 --> 00:04:11.350
(audience laughing)

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:12.460
But my first name-

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:14.240
Can you hear that right there?

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.765
Yes. It's my favorite
part of it (laughing)

00:04:17.765 --> 00:04:19.250
It is Andy's laugh.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:20.620
Did you hear that when you were on stage

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:21.510
actually performing?

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:22.940
I turned to him, I think,

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:23.850
cause specifically,

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.340
I heard a groan in the audience,

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:27.080
that was the boost of confidence I got

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.900
for the rest of the set was Andy's

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.180
approving laughter where
I was like, all right,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:32.013
I guess yeah.

00:04:32.013 --> 00:04:33.290
Cause it is-

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:38.010
Yeah, that joke, it is definitely
a hit or miss sometimes.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.140
It hit more than a miss,

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:40.727
but when people were just like..

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:42.640
right off the top,

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.582
you're already getting into (indistinct).

00:04:44.582 --> 00:04:46.102
(chuckles)

00:04:46.102 --> 00:04:48.820
(upbeat music)

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.210
I recently watched the Disney
animated feature Pocahontas,

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.840
because I'm a grown man
and no one can stop me

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.003
from doing whatever I want.

00:04:56.003 --> 00:04:59.003
(audience laughing)

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.040
However, I feel that movie should come

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.830
with a written apology.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:06.120
Firstly, the native Americans as a people

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:08.680
have some of the worst
genocide in human history.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:12.620
Some may say, "Hey Solomon,
what about the Holocaust?"

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:13.830
And I wouldn't take that away from anyone,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.060
the Holocaust was a
terrible, terrible tragedy.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:19.240
However, I have seen 10
or more Jewish people

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:20.426
in the same room.

00:05:20.426 --> 00:05:23.426
(audience laughing)

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.696
I haven't seen tentative
Americans in my life.

00:05:32.696 --> 00:05:36.779
(audience laughing and clapping)

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:42.920
You're threading a needle there.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:43.929
Yes.

00:05:43.929 --> 00:05:46.990
When you're basically trying
to qualify the difference

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:50.190
and quantify there between
both multiple holocausts here.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.910
I think for me there was sort of like

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:54.870
a need of comprehension of the weight

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:55.960
of what happened in this country.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.070
Sometimes I feel like whenever

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:59.610
we talk about native
Americans or in general,

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:01.100
whatever happens in this country,

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:04.080
we always find a way, some say
it's worse than other places,

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.260
and I'm like, no, it's not
worse than other places.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.190
It is just as bad here.

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:14.190
And I don't, I never expected
to do it with comedy,

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.910
but it's also, yeah,

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:19.230
it's always very weird
to me whenever anyone

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:21.130
talks about what goes on in
this country historically

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.560
as if it's always just like a less,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.310
less than aspect, and I kind of-

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.280
I do like talking about American history

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.230
in that way of like,

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:33.110
we should be deeply
ashamed of this. (laughs)

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:34.530
I definitely want the joke to exist,

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:35.840
but I also want a better joke

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.180
to have nice little layer of like,

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:38.900
you should not be happy
this ever happened.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:41.390
There's a national reckoning of history

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:43.100
and what, you know,

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.610
how white people have been benefited

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:49.530
from the persecution of a lot of others

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.700
and I think that's, you were in.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:53.960
This has been going on for a long time,

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:57.030
but just to see you in a
short five minutes said

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:00.590
being able to sort of hold
up a mirror and say like,

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:03.210
and the jokes have a way
of being a little bit silly

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:06.440
at times and you tag your joke,

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:07.990
there's a tag in here,

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:11.720
but it comes after the applause break.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:12.820
So you didn't even need it,

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:15.870
but yet it's still so great
that you had that built in.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:17.000
Yeah, I can't help it.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.395
Sometimes I'm too strict on the writing.

00:07:19.395 --> 00:07:22.096
(audience clapping)

00:07:22.096 --> 00:07:23.446
I used to live right her.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.413
I know what I'm saying,

00:07:25.413 --> 00:07:27.420
sometimes there's always
just a lot for people,

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:30.300
so I kind of have to be like, well,

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:31.720
here's a nice little down (indistinct),

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:34.350
be it's- I kind of had to
lift the weight a little bit.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.510
I can't just have people
walking out morose

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:37.930
every time I do a bit.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.150
Secondly, Pocahontas was a real person

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:41.370
in this nation's history.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:43.740
She looked nothing like
the Disney character

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.793
because she was hella busy being 12.

00:07:45.793 --> 00:07:49.620
(audience laughing)

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:51.290
She was sold to John Smith and his men,

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:52.930
suffered years of sexual assault,

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:54.490
witnessed the death of most of her tribe

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:55.560
and then shipped off to England

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:58.000
to die young death due to the smallpox.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.770
Now someone at the Disney corporation

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:02.197
caught wind of this tale.

00:08:02.197 --> 00:08:04.010
(audience laughing)

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:05.510
A complete 180,

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:08.950
like you're going down such a dark road

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:12.000
and yet so quickly turn and run around.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.650
Probably like the most
benefit I've ever had

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:15.590
doing comedy ever was doing it.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:19.680
There was like an understanding
of where people can be,

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:22.350
go away and not be okay
with you doing a bit,

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:24.700
and where you have to be
a certain amount of silly

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:26.300
and a certain amount of serious,

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:28.287
and it's-

00:08:28.287 --> 00:08:32.010
This joke has benefited me the
remainder of my career sense,

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.880
cause it's the best
conversation I ever had

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:35.940
of my salad coffee.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.420
And they thought, "Hey,

00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:39.565
how do we make this family friendly?"

00:08:39.565 --> 00:08:42.104
(audience laughing)

00:08:42.104 --> 00:08:42.937
"I got it,

00:08:42.937 --> 00:08:44.560
get rid of that part where
white people look bad

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:46.334
cause that is just gross."

00:08:46.334 --> 00:08:47.657
(audience laughing)

00:08:47.657 --> 00:08:49.740
"Then we'll throw in some singing,

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.667
some dancing and a raccoon, huh."

00:08:52.667 --> 00:08:56.750
(audience laughing and clapping)

00:09:03.547 --> 00:09:05.270
I apologize the only
impersonation I can do

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:06.836
is a blacker voice.

00:09:06.836 --> 00:09:07.990
(audience laughing)

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:10.360
Another moment where you're
just being a little silly.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:12.810
Yes. But also that
moment is because of you.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.387
I've never really listened to myself,

00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:16.110
I never listen to recorded
versions of myself,

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:18.470
and because of the recordings
that I sent to you,

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:20.840
I would have to listen
to this impersonation

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:23.180
of this fake self like, (JB laughs)

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:26.550
each person and I'm like, why is,

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.080
why do you sound blacker at this moment?

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.320
And I'm like, oh, that's
cause the only voice

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.033
that I ever, ever
attempted to impersonate,

00:09:32.033 --> 00:09:32.866
and it's like, oh yeah,

00:09:32.866 --> 00:09:34.821
that's that's all I got.
I don't really have-

00:09:34.821 --> 00:09:36.160
(JP laughing)

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:37.020
And then I realized, oh yeah,

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.140
my speaking voice is the whiter voice,

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.840
I forgot that. (both laughing)

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:45.080
Let's not forget the fact
that I forgot my own joke

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:46.023
halfway through.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:48.750
My cue cards are right in front of me,

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:51.770
with my actual joke to tell
me what I'm going to say.

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:53.130
And for some weird reason,

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.150
there's a point in time during my set,

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:58.990
where I realized the exact,
exactly what I was doing,

00:09:58.990 --> 00:09:59.823
which is like,

00:09:59.823 --> 00:10:02.556
oh, you're performing
in front of these people

00:10:02.556 --> 00:10:03.550
and it's gonna be on national TV,

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:08.230
and then my joke
literally just evaporated.

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:09.994
It didn't matter what was in front of me,

00:10:09.994 --> 00:10:10.827
then there was like-

00:10:10.827 --> 00:10:11.737
You're not the only one,

00:10:11.737 --> 00:10:12.737
it's happened.

00:10:12.737 --> 00:10:15.260
It's happened a handful of times.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:17.080
That's why I'm always
going to be so grateful

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:18.010
to that kind of audience

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:23.010
because the immediate
need to make me feel good

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:25.090
when I said, I forgot my own joke.

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:26.150
And like that was-

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:28.360
That was missing an
applause break from there,

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.320
cause it was an audience applauding me

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.057
to remember my own joke
instead of being like,

00:10:33.057 --> 00:10:34.930
"Hey you idiot. Why did you forget?"

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:35.817
And it was like, no, you've got like this.

00:10:35.817 --> 00:10:37.410
I'm like, I remember now.

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:40.070
The joy that came out of everybody's face.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:41.930
What other movies did they have?

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:45.830
This one is about a young
African princess named Sally,

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:49.660
who falls in love with a young
man named Thomas Jefferson.

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:50.585
stay on board.

00:10:50.585 --> 00:10:52.550
(audience laughing)

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:54.490
They get married and she helps him

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:56.463
write the declaration of independence,

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:58.610
with help from a friend,

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:00.619
Roscoe, the singing, frack chicken.

00:11:00.619 --> 00:11:01.452
(audience laughing)

00:11:01.452 --> 00:11:03.413
Now Tom the dancing watermelon.

